brainCUT bundles the following libraries. Each keeps its own license; the texts are in
node_modules/<package>/LICENSE after an install. Versions are the ones this repository is
pinned to in package.json, verified against the installed packages.

mediabunny 1.55.3 — demuxing, muxing and the WebCodecs wrappers
MPL-2.0
https://github.com/Vanilagy/mediabunny

pixi.js 8.20.1 — WebGL compositing
MIT
https://github.com/pixijs/pixijs

pixi-filters 6.1.5 — the effect filters built on top of it
MIT
https://github.com/pixijs/filters

gl-transitions 1.71.0 — the GLSL transitions
MIT, with one exception
https://github.com/gl-transitions/gl-transitions
The package is MIT, but the shaders inside it are not all on the same terms, so it is worth
being exact. Of the 125 shaders it ships, 123 say MIT in their header. One says BSD 2-Clause
(StereoViewer) and brainCUT does not ship it. The last one says BSD 3-Clause and brainCUT
does ship it: InvertedPageCurl.glsl, the transition the editor lists as "Page Curl", is

    Copyright (c) 2010 Hewlett-Packard Development Company, L.P. All rights reserved.

adapted by Sergey Kosarevsky. The copyright notice, the conditions under it and the author
line above it are all part of the shader source, and the shader source is what gets bundled
and compiled — so the notice travels with the code it covers, which is what BSD 3-Clause
asks for. Neither Hewlett-Packard nor its contributors endorse brainCUT.

Every other shader the editor compiles is MIT, and each one names its own author in its own
header. That credit is kept in the source in the same way.

immer 11.1.18 — immutable drafts and the patches the undo stack is built from
MIT
https://github.com/immerjs/immer

idb-keyval 6.3.0 — the IndexedDB wrapper projects and recents are stored through
Apache-2.0
https://github.com/jakearchibald/idb-keyval

tinykeys 4.0.0 — the keyboard shortcut bindings
MIT
https://github.com/jamiebuilds/tinykeys

bezier-easing 3.0.1 — cubic bezier curves behind the eased keyframes
MIT
https://github.com/gre/bezier-easing

gifenc 1.0.3 — GIF encoding and palette quantization
MIT
https://github.com/mattdesl/gifenc

@ffmpeg/ffmpeg 0.12.15 and @ffmpeg/util 0.12.2 — the loader for the converter
MIT
https://github.com/ffmpegwasm/ffmpeg.wasm

Two more are build tools rather than runtime dependencies, but what they produce ends up in
the deployed site, so they belong here too:

svelte 5 and @sveltejs/kit 2 — the framework, and the runtime it compiles into every
component. Both float on a caret range, so the installed version is whatever the lockfile
last resolved
MIT
https://github.com/sveltejs/svelte

tailwindcss 4 — the reset the theme is built on, compiled into app.css
MIT
https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss

Not part of this repository:

@ffmpeg/core — the ffmpeg build itself, WebAssembly
https://github.com/ffmpegwasm/ffmpeg.wasm
The core is not shipped with brainCUT, is not committed here, and is not a dependency in
package.json. It is downloaded from a CDN at runtime, once, and only when a file uses codecs
the browser cannot decode on its own. The default mirror is the @ffmpeg/core 0.12.10 build on
jsDelivr; the mirror is a preference and can be emptied, which turns conversion off entirely.

As the ffmpeg.wasm project states it, the core is built from FFmpeg, which is licensed under
the LGPL or the GPL depending on which components a given build was configured with, and
ffmpeg.wasm's own wrapper code is MIT. If you redistribute a build of the core yourself, or
ship brainCUT pointed at one you host, the FFmpeg license terms are yours to satisfy. See the
ffmpeg.wasm repository and https://ffmpeg.org/legal.html for the current wording.

Fonts are loaded at runtime from Google Fonts and are not redistributed here:
Instrument Serif, JetBrains Mono and Inter, all under the SIL Open Font License 1.1.
